[[:db/retract 17592186124094 :answer-field/choices 17592186124096] [:db/retract 17592186124094 :answer-field/choices 17592186124097] [:db/retract 17592186124094 :answer-field/choices 17592186124098] [:db/retract 17592186124094 :answer-field/choices 17592186124099] [:db/retract 17592186124094 :answer-field/choices 17592186124095] [:db/retract 17592186122354 :answer-field/choices 17592186122355] [:db/retract 17592186122354 :answer-field/choices 17592186122356] [:db/retract 17592186122354 :answer-field/choices 17592186122357] [:db/retract 17592186122354 :answer-field/choices 17592186122358] [:db/retract 17592186122354 :answer-field/choices 17592186122359] {:db/id 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-430b6986-f30c-500a-b5e9-219dd0f3ace5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-021b7b63-3816-5a7a-9f77-baf36bf193bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b98d6bad-fbc9-5978-8040-a1a35d995f54"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125249 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{xy}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124094 :answer-field/correct "answer-28207432-84ed-53b8-96d3-f43fe1450b2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-683e544e-ffb7-547d-87ef-b5301c6dac41" "answer-28207432-84ed-53b8-96d3-f43fe1450b2c" "answer-99330b27-129d-50df-97de-421d0fe9607b" "answer-21d33325-0f7c-59d5-9622-3be484ea7f04" "answer-fa4236d2-abd7-5e33-9757-638b2e5b9cc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-683e544e-ffb7-547d-87ef-b5301c6dac41" :answer/id #uuid "683e544e-ffb7-547d-87ef-b5301c6dac41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t}{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28207432-84ed-53b8-96d3-f43fe1450b2c" :answer/id #uuid "28207432-84ed-53b8-96d3-f43fe1450b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99330b27-129d-50df-97de-421d0fe9607b" :answer/id #uuid "99330b27-129d-50df-97de-421d0fe9607b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21d33325-0f7c-59d5-9622-3be484ea7f04" :answer/id #uuid "21d33325-0f7c-59d5-9622-3be484ea7f04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa4236d2-abd7-5e33-9757-638b2e5b9cc5" :answer/id #uuid "fa4236d2-abd7-5e33-9757-638b2e5b9cc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2}{{t}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b" :question/id #uuid "240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95051" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-47babdd0-5f4c-5c9a-9ce1-4d934697545f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-47babdd0-5f4c-5c9a-9ce1-4d934697545f" :answer-field/id #uuid "47babdd0-5f4c-5c9a-9ce1-4d934697545f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" "answer-4b40ea9e-cf34-5238-9134-0581b982e962" "answer-0c059460-c4c2-55a2-8585-7e51d8edae78" "answer-e85749fe-f788-582a-a8ba-6a6fb947faa2" "answer-47522fd9-62bd-5474-804d-67ac7a770b4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/id #uuid "c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b40ea9e-cf34-5238-9134-0581b982e962" :answer/id #uuid "4b40ea9e-cf34-5238-9134-0581b982e962" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c059460-c4c2-55a2-8585-7e51d8edae78" :answer/id #uuid "0c059460-c4c2-55a2-8585-7e51d8edae78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e85749fe-f788-582a-a8ba-6a6fb947faa2" :answer/id #uuid "e85749fe-f788-582a-a8ba-6a6fb947faa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{cos}^{3}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47522fd9-62bd-5474-804d-67ac7a770b4b" :answer/id #uuid "47522fd9-62bd-5474-804d-67ac7a770b4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{sin}^{4}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124959 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+e\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+yz+xz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+yz+xz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+yz+xz)\\,k \\\\  & =(y+z)\\,i+(x+z)\\,j+(y+x)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y={e}^{t},$ and $z=e$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =({e}^{t}+e)\\,i+(t+e)\\,j+({e}^{t}+t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e)\\,k \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(0)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(({e}^{t}+e)\\,i+(t+e)\\,j+({e}^{t}+t)\\,k)\\cdot (i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(0)\\,k) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}+e)\\cdot 1+(t+e)\\cdot {e}^{t}+({e}^{t}+t)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+e+t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122354 :answer-field/correct "answer-88d607c8-fe3c-5a8f-95e2-26b4560aaab5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f8c2a6a-498f-5431-bf0a-f09364aeca5b" "answer-2d6b5248-8092-5223-a861-76c990d98151" "answer-88d607c8-fe3c-5a8f-95e2-26b4560aaab5" "answer-55795f5b-54c2-5a78-8f3f-d7bc330a0c55" "answer-9a2dcd24-b4f5-5abd-afe6-bb8c91b06230"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f8c2a6a-498f-5431-bf0a-f09364aeca5b" :answer/id #uuid "3f8c2a6a-498f-5431-bf0a-f09364aeca5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{t}+2e+t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d6b5248-8092-5223-a861-76c990d98151" :answer/id #uuid "2d6b5248-8092-5223-a861-76c990d98151" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(1+2t){e}^{t}+e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88d607c8-fe3c-5a8f-95e2-26b4560aaab5" :answer/id #uuid "88d607c8-fe3c-5a8f-95e2-26b4560aaab5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55795f5b-54c2-5a78-8f3f-d7bc330a0c55" :answer/id #uuid "55795f5b-54c2-5a78-8f3f-d7bc330a0c55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(t+1){e}^{t}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a2dcd24-b4f5-5abd-afe6-bb8c91b06230" :answer/id #uuid "9a2dcd24-b4f5-5abd-afe6-bb8c91b06230" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+t{e}^{t}+e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-430b6986-f30c-500a-b5e9-219dd0f3ace5" :question/id #uuid "430b6986-f30c-500a-b5e9-219dd0f3ace5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167463" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,i+xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,j+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=2t$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88e49b12-6312-563d-bb5a-a9112af44abf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88e49b12-6312-563d-bb5a-a9112af44abf" :answer-field/id #uuid "88e49b12-6312-563d-bb5a-a9112af44abf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d6d49e7-278c-5501-995f-670dd3f19484" "answer-d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" "answer-3a36a033-d9b0-5aa7-a7f4-b5451390ac7a" "answer-60dab585-b4f4-59ea-8d98-9a5182b158eb" "answer-ff848b94-7fca-53f6-95e5-3ab48c4f01c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d6d49e7-278c-5501-995f-670dd3f19484" :answer/id #uuid "8d6d49e7-278c-5501-995f-670dd3f19484" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2+2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" :answer/id #uuid "d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a36a033-d9b0-5aa7-a7f4-b5451390ac7a" :answer/id #uuid "3a36a033-d9b0-5aa7-a7f4-b5451390ac7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60dab585-b4f4-59ea-8d98-9a5182b158eb" :answer/id #uuid "60dab585-b4f4-59ea-8d98-9a5182b158eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff848b94-7fca-53f6-95e5-3ab48c4f01c2" :answer/id #uuid "ff848b94-7fca-53f6-95e5-3ab48c4f01c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-021b7b63-3816-5a7a-9f77-baf36bf193bb" :question/id #uuid "021b7b63-3816-5a7a-9f77-baf36bf193bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-65864" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da5c7c2a-000b-5483-8ada-2cf63e301ba8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da5c7c2a-000b-5483-8ada-2cf63e301ba8" :answer-field/id #uuid "da5c7c2a-000b-5483-8ada-2cf63e301ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" "answer-05ab9250-28af-5908-8d6f-09833d521f51" "answer-bd8e760c-2dd0-5dcf-8836-3c5e2deced71" "answer-cc6cc197-cc16-54f4-b4ca-9fa9bd9e20c8" "answer-de9daefe-3ccb-54d5-9516-f29765d78d78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" :answer/id #uuid "67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05ab9250-28af-5908-8d6f-09833d521f51" :answer/id #uuid "05ab9250-28af-5908-8d6f-09833d521f51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd8e760c-2dd0-5dcf-8836-3c5e2deced71" :answer/id #uuid "bd8e760c-2dd0-5dcf-8836-3c5e2deced71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc6cc197-cc16-54f4-b4ca-9fa9bd9e20c8" :answer/id #uuid "cc6cc197-cc16-54f4-b4ca-9fa9bd9e20c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de9daefe-3ccb-54d5-9516-f29765d78d78" :answer/id #uuid "de9daefe-3ccb-54d5-9516-f29765d78d78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124960 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+3xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t+1,-t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+3xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x+3yz,3xz,3xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=t+1,$ and $z=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2({t}^{2})+3(t+1)(-t),3({t}^{2})(-t),3({t}^{2})(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}-3{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(-{t}^{2}-3t)\\cdot 2t+(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot 1+(3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-8{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =-8{(1)}^{3}-9{(1)}^{2}=-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122360 :answer-field/correct 17592186122361 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b98d6bad-fbc9-5978-8040-a1a35d995f54" :question/id #uuid "b98d6bad-fbc9-5978-8040-a1a35d995f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-36234" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,2t⟩$ at the point $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6ea0794-709e-5625-aee2-f32cf13014aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6ea0794-709e-5625-aee2-f32cf13014aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ea0794-709e-5625-aee2-f32cf13014aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer-field/choices ["answer-869d2a2c-eca1-5a81-9c8c-bd38ea652d36" "answer-e898d08a-c5cb-5c48-be98-c6dd2a755c94" "answer-9980bb90-a6ef-51f4-88b3-4812a2e11e8e" "answer-4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" "answer-76ff76d1-a44d-5785-aeb6-f49cd08b8d01"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-869d2a2c-eca1-5a81-9c8c-bd38ea652d36" :answer/id #uuid "869d2a2c-eca1-5a81-9c8c-bd38ea652d36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e898d08a-c5cb-5c48-be98-c6dd2a755c94" :answer/id #uuid "e898d08a-c5cb-5c48-be98-c6dd2a755c94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9980bb90-a6ef-51f4-88b3-4812a2e11e8e" :answer/id #uuid "9980bb90-a6ef-51f4-88b3-4812a2e11e8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer/id #uuid "4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76ff76d1-a44d-5785-aeb6-f49cd08b8d01" :answer/id #uuid "76ff76d1-a44d-5785-aeb6-f49cd08b8d01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125835 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the function \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{r}) = f(x,y) = xy^2,\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t) \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nas input. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right]?$\n\nTo do this, we use the **multivariable chain rule in vector form**, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now apply this to our functions. First, we compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(f(x,y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(f(x,y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{2},2xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2},2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =-{sin}^{3}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125749 :tutorial/content "We can consider $f(\\mathbf{r}(t))$ as the values taken by the function $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$ So, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))]\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the **rate of change of $f$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$**\n\nFurthermore, if $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is a level curve $f(x,y)=c,$ then by the chain rule we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =c \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(f(r(t))) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(c) \\\\ ∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can conclude that the gradient vector $\\nabla f$ is orthogonal to every level curve of $f.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125836 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for a function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x_1(t), x_2(t), \\ldots, x_m(t) \\right\\rangle$ is a vector-valued function of $m$ variables, is the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nHow does this chain rule in vector form relate to the chain rule we have seen before, which states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d f} {\\textrm d t} \t= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_1}{\\textrm d t}  \n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_2}{\\textrm d t} + \\dots + \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_m}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet's expand $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)$ to see. Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))=⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{\\text{d}t}+⋯+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey are exactly the same, just different notations!\n\n**Note**: If we replace $\\mathbf{r}$ with a single-valued function $g(t),$ we get back the chain rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(g(t))] & =∇f(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124728 :activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
